WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
URAI Labs and Chalk & Cheese present: The Unknown.
One million numbers. Zero counterexamples in our finite tests.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The Collatz mystery: for a positive whole number,
if even, divide by two; if odd, multiply by three and add one.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Example: starting at 27, the repeated steps eventually reach 1.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Collatz test: one million starting values; zero counterexamples.
A finite test does not prove the conjecture.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Within the tested range, 837,799 takes 524 steps to reach 1.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Goldbach: 499,999 even numbers from 4 through one million checked.
No counterexamples were found in that range.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Twin primes: 8,169 pairs found through one million.
Whether there are infinitely many is still unknown.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Our software ran finite computational checks.
An infinite claim requires mathematical proof.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Next up: the Riemann Hypothesis.
Levent Alpöge, working with Claude, developed a Lean-checked proof.
The Lean and Mathlib communities certified its formal verification.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.250
URAI Labs × Chalk & Cheese — THE UNKNOWN.
Explore. Question. Verify. Discover.
Finite checks are not general proofs.
